[Event "Queen's Indian: 4.g3"]

{The Queen's Indian (3...b6) controls e4 with the b7-bishop.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... e6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
3. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
3... b6 {[%csl Gb7] The Queen's Indian. The pawn on b6 controls a5 and c5.}
4. g3 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g3 controls f4 and h4.}
4... Ba6 {Hits c4. Develops the bishop to a6, where it eyes the pawn on c4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
 (4... Bb7 {Classical development. Fianchettoes the bishop on b7, where it rakes the long diagonal through c6, d5 and e4.}
5. Bg2 {Long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5... Be7 {Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls d6 and supports the knight on f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
)
5. b3 {Defends c4. The pawn on b3 controls a4 and supports the pawn on c4.}
5... Bb4+ {Checks. It is check, so the reply is forced. Develops the bishop to b4, where it controls c5 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6. Bd2 {Blocks. Develops the bishop to d2, where it controls e3 and f4. It attacks the bishop on b4, which is undefended.}
6... Be7 {Moves the bishop out of attack to e7.}
7. Bg2 {Long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7... c6 {Prepares ...d5. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and d5.}
8. Bc3 {The bishop goes to c3 and supports the pawn on d4.}
8... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
9. Ne5 {Central. The knight goes to e5.}
9... Nfd7 {Challenges. Regroups the knight to d7 and eyes the knight on e5. White's last move put a knight on e5, which this now pressures.} *

[Event "Queen's Indian: Petrosian & 4.e3"]

{4.a3 stops ...Bb4.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... e6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
3. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
3... b6 {QID. The pawn on b6 controls a5 and c5.}
4. a3 {The Petrosian. The pawn on a3 controls b4.}
 (4. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 controls f4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f1.}
4... Bb7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on b7, where it rakes the long diagonal through c6, d5 and e4.}
5. Bd3 {Develops the bishop to d3, where it controls e4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... Bd6 {Develops the bishop to d6, where it controls e5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7. b3 {The pawn on b3 controls a4 and supports the pawn on c4.}
7... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
)
 (4. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
4... Bb4 {Pins. Develops the bishop to b4, where it controls c5 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. Bg5 {Pins. Develops the bishop to g5, where it controls f4 and eyes the knight on f6.}
5... Bb7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on b7, where it rakes the long diagonal through c6, d5 and e4.}
6. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 controls f4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f1.}
6... h6 {Asks. The pawn on h6 eyes the bishop on g5.}
7. Bh4 {Keeps the pin. Moves the bishop out of attack to h4.}
7... g5 {Breaks the pin. Gains space on the kingside; the pawn on g5 takes f4 and h4 away from the opponent. It attacks the bishop on h4.}
8. Bg3 {Moves the bishop out of attack to g3 and controls e5.}
8... Ne4 {Central. The knight goes to e4. White's last move put a bishop on g3, which this now pressures.}
)
4... Bb7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on b7, where it rakes the long diagonal through c6, d5 and e4.}
5. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
5... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
6. cxd5 {Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
6... Nxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
7. Qc2 {Prepares e4. The queen goes to c2 and controls e4.}
7... Nd7 {Develops the knight to d7, where it controls e5.} *
